WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:06.750
So one of the most important sort of components
of social democracy, which is the political

00:00:06.750 --> 00:00:13.110
ideology that I most align with, is that we
see certain areas of life, certain areas of

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:20.140
an economy as deserving of being outside of
the for profit system. A social democracy

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:28.189
is capitalism, but it is capitalism with limits.
It's capitalism with the fruits of that system

00:00:28.189 --> 00:00:35.239
reinvested significantly in making sure that
no one falls too low in terms of their status

00:00:35.239 --> 00:00:41.700
in, in the world and the economy in, in society.
Uh, and there are certain areas that often

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:46.499
come up as deserving to be outside of that
for-profit system. Health care is very often

00:00:46.499 --> 00:00:52.329
one. Education is very often one and one that
has been more discussed lately but, but less

00:00:52.329 --> 00:00:58.429
frequently is doing something about housing.
How should we deal with housing and with so

00:00:58.429 --> 00:01:01.780
much negative news, like a lot of this stuff
we're talking about is bad.

00:01:01.780 --> 00:01:06.180
Things that have happened, I have today just
a really great policy proposal to discuss

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:10.340
from Bernie Sanders relating to housing. It
says housing for all plan that he released

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:15.570
yesterday. And there's a view about housing
that is we should totally de commodify housing,

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:20.740
completely remove it from the marketplace,
set up some kind of land value tax that would

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:25.571
equal the rental value of properties so that
people can still own private property. We're

00:01:25.571 --> 00:01:31.229
not saying no more ownership of private property,
a real estate that, that being a but seeking

00:01:31.229 --> 00:01:37.439
rent on property will be pointless because
whatever amount of rent you earn, you will

00:01:37.439 --> 00:01:41.049
have to pay a land value tax equivalent to
that. Now potentially you would still get

00:01:41.049 --> 00:01:45.590
the value of owning the property, but you
would get that either way. Uh, now there are

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:49.539
also that may that understandably will sound
extreme to some people in the audience.

00:01:49.539 --> 00:01:54.729
There are less extreme versions than totally
socializing ownership of real estate, of,

00:01:54.729 --> 00:01:59.759
of all housing. And uh, the truth is that
if you hear me talking about this and you

00:01:59.759 --> 00:02:03.939
say, David, you're not going far enough. We
should de commodify all housing and put a

00:02:03.939 --> 00:02:08.480
land value tax equivalent to the rental value
of any property. That's not going to happen

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:12.540
anytime soon. I think most people would agree.
Whether you like that or not, that's a debate,

00:02:12.540 --> 00:02:16.950
but it's not happening anytime soon. So the,
the immediate thing that we should look at

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:22.010
doing is what is actually doable, which is
socially democratic programs that will seriously

00:02:22.010 --> 00:02:26.401
deal with homelessness and the rising cost
of living across the United States. Bernie

00:02:26.401 --> 00:02:30.760
Sanders has now released such a proposal and
it's a very solid one and I want to talk about

00:02:30.760 --> 00:02:36.890
it with you today. The premise is that we
have a situation where it's frankly that we

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:38.860
have homelessness in the United States.

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:44.630
We're a rich country. We have the money financially.
We can end homelessness, homelessness in the

00:02:44.630 --> 00:02:50.629
United States, we should consider homes or
place to live or right. Cost of living is

00:02:50.629 --> 00:02:55.489
out of control, rent seeking and gentrification
at the hands of wealthy developers and landowners

00:02:55.489 --> 00:03:01.150
and rich people. It hurts so many people and
Bernie Sanders plan deals with a lot of this.

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:04.230
Now I'm just going to give you some of the
highlights. I know some people will say, I

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:07.950
missed some important elements of this. There
are more than 30 provisions, but I want to

00:03:07.950 --> 00:03:14.129
focus on some of the important ones from Bernie's
housing. For all proposal. There will be one,

00:03:14.129 --> 00:03:18.709
nearly one and a half trillion dollars spent
over 10 years to build and renovate nearly

00:03:18.709 --> 00:03:24.140
seven and a half million affordable housing
units in mixed income developments as well

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:31.099
as $400 billion for 2 million mixed income
social housing units for desegregation and

00:03:31.099 --> 00:03:32.099
integration.

00:03:32.099 --> 00:03:37.680
Boston actually does some of this where affordable
housing units are, uh, placed the, you might

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:44.150
have a 12 unit, um, development and a certain
number of those are designated affordable,

00:03:44.150 --> 00:03:49.040
which helps to integrate rather than segregate
people on the basis of income. It's not perfect,

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:54.909
but it does do that to a great degree. Uh,
Bernie would expand. USDA is section five,

00:03:54.909 --> 00:04:00.180
one five by $500 million to build affordable
units in rural areas as well as protect affordable

00:04:00.180 --> 00:04:04.549
units, uh, existing affordable units. Very
important. You know, we talked about how can

00:04:04.549 --> 00:04:08.250
a Democrats appeal to rural voters. I'm going
to deal with this more on tomorrow's show

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:14.550
as well. This is a great idea to go along
those lines. Indian housing block grants will

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:20.540
be increased to $3 billion. In total funding.
There will be $70 billion allocated to repair

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:25.130
and update public housing including high speed
broadband, which is important.

00:04:25.130 --> 00:04:30.600
We've talked about broadband as a, uh, having
it versus not having it is, is increasingly

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:35.980
a segregating factor economically. Uh, the
fair cloth amendment will be repealed. That

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:43.490
amendment prevents building new public housing
in certain situations via a g n d we will,

00:04:43.490 --> 00:04:49.280
uh, the green new deal that is there will
be the electrification and decarbonization

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:55.510
of public housing, which is both a good for
the climate and also good for the people who

00:04:55.510 --> 00:05:00.230
are in public housing. Uh, section eight rental
assistance will be fully funded at the rate

00:05:00.230 --> 00:05:05.450
of $410 billion over 10 years. Make sense?
There will be a strengthening of the fair

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:12.440
housing act and the addition of a nondiscrimination
law. This a small area fair market rent rule

00:05:12.440 --> 00:05:18.890
will be expanded and there will be $2 billion
via the USDA combined with $6 billion via

00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:23.680
HUD for a first time home buyer program. First
time home buyer programs have existed for

00:05:23.680 --> 00:05:28.050
a long time, but the idea is let's increase
it, let's, um, strengthen it in order to deal

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:32.750
with the high cost of living that still keeps
people who are just a little bit too wealthy

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:35.660
to qualify for some of these programs from
buying.

00:05:35.660 --> 00:05:40.510
We can enable them to, uh, become first time
home buyers as well, and then end the mass

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:44.180
sale of mortgages to Wall Street. This is
something a lot of people don't even know

00:05:44.180 --> 00:05:47.470
happens, but very often there are lots of
banks who you will take out a mortgage with

00:05:47.470 --> 00:05:54.371
them and very soon thereafter they will sell
the mortgage to a big Wall Street firm. This

00:05:54.371 --> 00:05:59.450
is an extraordinarily speculative activity.
It doesn't benefit the consumer. Uh, and many

00:05:59.450 --> 00:06:03.780
people don't even know it happens because
often the originating bank will continue servicing

00:06:03.780 --> 00:06:11.280
the loan. So what's, what's not often discussed
and may not be obvious to those slamming Bernie

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:16.560
for wanting to quote, spend your money with
a proposal like this because people haven't

00:06:16.560 --> 00:06:21.960
earned it. As we often hear from right wingers,
there is a morality argument to make, which

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:24.970
is that we are an extraordinarily rich country.

00:06:24.970 --> 00:06:28.890
Ending homelessness is merely a matter of
deciding to end it. Not a matter of having

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:32.830
to figure out really complicated ways to do
it. We know exactly how to do it. Let's say

00:06:32.830 --> 00:06:41.710
you don't care about the morality. Homelessness
has insane economic costs that are quote,

00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:46.780
socialized among all of us. Homeless people
are more likely for a variety of reasons to

00:06:46.780 --> 00:06:53.040
end up in the emergency room uninsured that
costs you money. Homeless people are more

00:06:53.040 --> 00:06:58.800
likely to get arrested for marginal crimes
that are associated with homelessness and

00:06:58.800 --> 00:07:04.640
that filled jails costing us tax dollars.
Um, that it's, it's, we could go into many

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:10.280
examples of that and we have, but that costs
you money. Homeless people have more difficulty

00:07:10.280 --> 00:07:15.130
finding jobs. Not having an address makes
it harder to find a job. Not having a stable

00:07:15.130 --> 00:07:19.520
place to live makes it harder to keep a job
and to do well in that job.

00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:24.190
Meaning that homeless people don't earn wages,
meaning they don't pay taxes into state and

00:07:24.190 --> 00:07:30.210
local governments, which is not good for any
of us. Being homeless is really expensive

00:07:30.210 --> 00:07:34.120
for society and providing not just a home,
but I would argue also a medical care as we've

00:07:34.120 --> 00:07:38.881
talked about for the last 10 years on the
show is cheaper for you in the long run. If

00:07:38.881 --> 00:07:43.110
you don't care about the morality of it. If
you don't care about the people as people,

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:47.160
then maybe you care about the finances of
it and it will save you money to do something

00:07:47.160 --> 00:07:51.320
like what Bernie Sanders is proposing here.
The other financial thing here is that lots

00:07:51.320 --> 00:07:56.310
of these components that Bernie proposes are
what we call demand side stimulus, construction,

00:07:56.310 --> 00:08:01.200
energy retrofits, service providers of all
kinds are involved, companies of all kinds

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:07.230
are involved. That is an entire additional
front of economic stimulus that is in addition

00:08:07.230 --> 00:08:09.230
to everything else that we're talking about.

00:08:09.230 --> 00:08:14.970
Uh, and so on. All Fronts. This really makes
sense. It's a great plan. No plan is perfect,

00:08:14.970 --> 00:08:21.790
but I hope that other candidates will develop
similar plans and uh, that if we get a Democratic

00:08:21.790 --> 00:08:24.550
president replacing Donald Trump, that this
is one of the first things they deal with

00:08:24.550 --> 00:08:28.510
because it will be good not only for the homeless
people that will directly benefit. This will

00:08:28.510 --> 00:08:31.752
be good for the economy. This'll be good for
neighborhood safety. This will be good in

00:08:31.752 --> 00:08:31.770
every way that we can imagine.

